   "text": {
    "L1": "Gajaraj was a royal elephant, and Buntee was a small stray dog. They were unlikely friends because they were very different.",
    "L2": "A common myth says friends must be alike. This story shows that the idea is not true.",
    "L3": "Gajaraj had a kind mahout who gave him food and a bath. But a caretaker is not the same as a friend to play and share with.",
    "L4": "When Buntee came to the stable, Gajaraj pushed some of his own food towards him. This kind action showed that he wanted a friend.",
    "L5": "Gajaraj gave Buntee rides, and they spent time together. Their friendship grew through sharing and care.",
    "L6": "When Buntee was taken away, both friends refused food. They were missing each other.",
    "L7": "The farmer took off Buntee's rope and set him free. Buntee thanked him, then ran back to Gajaraj.",
    "L8": "The message is clear: very different beings can be true friends. At the end, the mahout and the farmer became friends too."
   },
